WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:02.280
JENNY BROWN: Today,
we're wrapping up Next

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.730
and talking about confidential
computing and machine learning.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.000
I'm Jenny, and welcome to "This
Week in Cloud," where we serve

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.180
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.060
If you've been hanging out with
us for the past nine weeks,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.360
we've worked on an actual ton
of content with Next OnAir.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.120
It's been a lot of
fun, but it can also

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.550
be hard to keep track of all the
different topics we've covered.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.000
That's why we've compiled a
full list of announcements

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.990
so you can review and get
more on all your favorites.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.210
No matter how you want
to use Google Cloud,

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.560
your data is your data.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.450
We're always looking
to improve security.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.320
And now, we've announced
confidential computing.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.210
This keeps your data
even more secure

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.290
by keeping data encrypted in
memory and away from the CPU.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.420
We've recently launched
confidential VMs

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.250
and are now expanding
that security

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.370
to confidential GKE nodes.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.010
Building ML and AI apps
can take a lot of work,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:58.470
but there's more
tooling than ever

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:01.350
to help you capture
the power of ML.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.990
Dale Markowitz,
applied AI engineer,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.630
has put together a list
of helpful tools and tips

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.870
to how to spend more time on
your application and less time

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:11.850
on tweaking your models.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.400
We love good documentation,
as it can help

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.380
make a hard project possible.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.240
Code samples are always welcome.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.250
And now, it's even
easier to use them,

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.770
thanks to interactive
documentation.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.810
You can put in your
specific values,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.410
and the code samples will update
to match exactly what you need.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.960
And you can even
preview actual results.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.050
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.620
or see a full list, check out
the link in the description.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.220
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.980
Leave us a comment or tweet us,
and we'll see it in the Cloud.

